From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: drain the card status the HS400 sweep dirties

Three cold boots of the `mmc-sunxi-drop-the-hs400-command-settle-workaround` kernel on the 8 GiB board all end the same way
:

  sunxi-mmc 4022000.mmc: HS400: data-strobe window 1..44 (44 wide), using 23
  mmc1: unexpected status 0x400900 after switch
  mmc1: new HS400 MMC card at address 0001

The card initialises, read and write verify by md5, and no error is logged
under load -- but the warning is there every single time, so it is not noise.

0x400900 decodes as CURRENT_STATE=4 (tran), READY_FOR_DATA, and bit 22,
R1_ILLEGAL_COMMAND. mmc_switch_status_error() prints it because
R1_STATUS(x) = x & 0xFFF9A000 keeps exactly that bit; R1_SWITCH_ERROR is not
set, which is why initialisation continues.

The card is right and the dirt is ours. The data-strobe sweep in
->execute_hs400_tuning fails on purpose for every delay outside the window --
20 "send stop command failed" lines per boot, likewise identical across all
three -- and each failed data transfer makes sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop()
issue a CMD12 the card cannot honour in the state it is then in. That latches
ILLEGAL_COMMAND, R1 error bits are cleared only by being read, and the core
reads the status immediately after the hook returns: mmc.c calls
->execute_hs400_tuning and then mmc_switch_status() a few lines below.

So read the status once at the end of the sweep and throw it away. The
alternative would be to teach the layer above to ignore a status bit that is
genuinely set, which is how the command-settle workaround removed in `mmc-sunxi-drop-the-hs400-command-settle-workaround` came
about in the first place.

Placed after the sweep and before the "no delay passes" exit, so the card is
also left clean on the failing path.

CORRECTED 2026-09-19: that sentence used to say "the path where the core falls
back to HS200". The core does not fall back; before the HS400 fallback fix the failing path cost
the whole card. The placement is right for the reason given -- the card is left
clean either way -- but the reason was described with a mechanism that does not
exist, and it is deleted rather than struck through.

Hardware test: the same three cold boots plus read/write/md5, with the warning
gone and window, pick and throughput unchanged.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -1375,6 +1375,32 @@
  * bus that cannot frame a block. That is the intended behaviour: a slower eMMC
  * is a nuisance, a silently corrupting one is not.
  */
+
+/*
+ * Read the card status once and discard it.
+ *
+ * The sweep above fails on purpose for every delay outside the window, and each
+ * failed data transfer makes sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop() send a CMD12 that
+ * the card cannot honour in the state it is then in. The card records that as
+ * ILLEGAL_COMMAND, and R1 error bits are only cleared by being read. Since the
+ * core calls mmc_switch_status() immediately after this hook returns, leaving
+ * them latched means it reports our own leftovers as "unexpected status
+ * 0x400900 after switch" -- R1_STATUS() masks exactly bit 22 out of that value.
+ * It is only a warning, but it is a warning about dirt we made, so clean it up
+ * here rather than teach the layer above to ignore it.
+ */
+static void sunxi_mmc_drain_card_status(struct mmc_host *mmc,
+					struct mmc_card *card)
+{
+	struct mmc_command cmd = {};
+
+	cmd.opcode = MMC_SEND_STATUS;
+	cmd.arg = card->rca << 16;
+	cmd.flags = MMC_RSP_R1 | MMC_CMD_AC;
+
+	mmc_wait_for_cmd(mmc, &cmd, 0);
+}
+
 static int sunxi_mmc_execute_hs400_tuning(struct mmc_host *mmc,
 					  struct mmc_card *card)
 {
@@ -1411,6 +1437,8 @@
 		}
 	}
 
+	sunxi_mmc_drain_card_status(mmc, card);
+
 	if (!best_len) {
 		dev_err(mmc_dev(mmc),
 			"HS400: no data-strobe delay passes, falling back\n");
